Each pod in a StatefulSet — like mongo-0, mongo-1, mongo-2 —
should have its own independent disk (volume) so that:

If a pod restarts → its data remains safe

If a new pod is created → it gets its own clean storage

If pod mongo-0 is deleted → its volume is not reused by mongo-1

🧱 How Kubernetes Manages Volumes per Pod in a StatefulSet
Step 1️⃣ — You define volumeClaimTemplates in the YAML
volumeClaimTemplates:
- metadata:
    name: mongo-data
  spec:
    accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
    resources:
      requests:
        storage: 5Gi
This means:

“For each pod in this StatefulSet, automatically create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) of 5 GiB named mongo-data.”

Step 2️⃣ — Kubernetes automatically creates one PVC per Pod
When StatefulSet runs with replicas: 3, it does this:

Pod Name	PVC Name	PV (Persistent Volume)
mongo-0	mongo-data-mongo-0	EBS volume (5 GiB)
mongo-1	mongo-data-mongo-1	EBS volume (5 GiB)
mongo-2	mongo-data-mongo-2	EBS volume (5 GiB)
✅ Each PVC is unique to that pod.
✅ PVCs are bound to PersistentVolumes (PV), which are usually AWS EBS disks in EKS.

Step 3️⃣ — Each Pod Mounts Its Own PVC
In the container section:

volumeMounts:
- name: mongo-data
  mountPath: /data/db
When mongo-0 starts:

It mounts mongo-data-mongo-0 → /data/db

When mongo-1 starts:

It mounts mongo-data-mongo-1 → /data/db

So each pod sees /data/db — but backed by its own EBS volume.

Step 4️⃣ — What Happens When Pods Restart?
Let’s say mongo-1 crashes.
Kubernetes does:

Recreates mongo-1 (same pod name)

Reuses the same PVC mongo-data-mongo-1

Pod mounts the same EBS volume → data is still there

✅ Data persists
✅ Pod identity (mongo-1) remains stable
✅ Perfect for databases, queues, etc.

Step 5️⃣ — What If You Scale Up or Down?
Scale up to 4 pods:

New pod mongo-3 is created

New PVC mongo-data-mongo-3 is automatically created

New EBS volume provisioned

Scale down to 2 pods:

Pod mongo-2 is terminated

PVC mongo-data-mongo-2 remains (data safe)

You can later scale back up → it will reuse the same PVC

