Let me explain clearly.

✅ 1. Can we create a StatefulSet without a Service?
Technically YES — Kubernetes will not stop you.
You can apply a StatefulSet YAML even if no Service exists.

But…

⚠️ Pods will still be created
⚠️ PVCs will still be created

…however, the StatefulSet will NOT give you stable DNS hostnames, which is the entire reason we use StatefulSet for stateful apps.

❌ 2. Why StatefulSet without a headless Service is almost useless
A StatefulSet needs a headless Service (clusterIP: None) to provide stable DNS names.

With the headless service, pods get DNS like:

pod-0.pg-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
pod-1.pg-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
pod-2.pg-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
This allows other pods/services to connect to exact members (ex: Kafka broker-1, Cassandra node-0, Postgres primary).

🔹 Without headless service → NO predictable DNS.
Pods will have random IPs that change on restart.

🔑 3. Why does StatefulSet NEED stable DNS names?
Because most stateful apps depend on node identity:

Examples:
Kafka broker IDs map to pod ordinal numbers

Cassandra seeds are fixed nodes

MongoDB replica-set members need fixed hostnames

ZooKeeper nodes need consistent identity for quorum

Postgres replication (primary → replicas) needs stable hostnames

If DNS keeps changing, the cluster breaks.

StatefulSet gives stable identity by:

Feature	How it works
Stable pod name	mydb-0, mydb-1, mydb-2
Stable DNS	Provided through HEADLESS SERVICE
Stable volume	PVC per pod (mydb-data-mydb-0)
Ordered startup	0 → 1 → 2
Ordered shutdown	reverse order
But DNS stability only works when a headless Service exists.

⭐ 4. Why HEADLESS service? Why not normal Service?
A normal service load-balances traffic randomly across pods → NOT useful for stateful apps.

A headless service (clusterIP: None) does:

✔ Exposes individual pod IPs
✔ Creates individual DNS records
✔ No load balancing — clients connect directly to specific pods

Example DNS:

nslookup pg-headless
-> returns pg-0, pg-1, pg-2 individually
This is exactly what databases message queues require.

🙋 So why Kubernetes documentation recommends it?
Because for StatefulSets, headless service is the mechanism that allows Kubernetes to “bind” the stable identity to the pod via DNS.

Official Kubernetes doc summary:

A headless service is required to manage the network identity of StatefulSet pods.

✔ FINAL answer (simple)
Yes, you can create a StatefulSet without a headless Service…
But then the pods will NOT get stable DNS names
…which breaks most stateful applications.
So in real DevOps and production, we always create:

StatefulSet

Headless Service (clusterIP: None)

PVCs (via volumeClaimTemplates)

These three together make StatefulSet behave correctly.

