You said:
How CoreDNS internally creates these records??
ChatGPT said:
Great question — understanding how CoreDNS internally creates DNS records for a StatefulSet + Headless Service will deepen your Kubernetes networking knowledge.

Let's explain step-by-step, from inside CoreDNS.

🧠 How CoreDNS Internally Creates DNS Records
CoreDNS does not scan pods by itself.
Instead, CoreDNS uses a plugin called kubernetes plugin, which dynamically watches the Kubernetes API.

This plugin makes DNS entries from three Kubernetes objects:

Kubernetes Object	DNS Created By CoreDNS
Services	Service DNS (ClusterIP or Headless)
Endpoints / EndpointSlices	Pod-level DNS (A records)
Pods (if enabled)	Optional pod DNS
🔍 Mechanism Step by Step
1️⃣ CoreDNS watches Kubernetes API for changes
CoreDNS contains this plugin in /etc/coredns/Corefile:

kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {
    pods insecure
    fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa
}
This means:

CoreDNS subscribes to Kubernetes API (watch)

It listens for:

Services

Endpoints / EndpointSlices

Pods

It stores this in memory — DNS is fully dynamic.

2️⃣ You create a Headless Service
Example:

clusterIP: None
When CoreDNS sees clusterIP: None:

❗ It does NOT create a service VIP
Instead, it creates:

SRV and A records for EACH pod in the service.
3️⃣ Kubernetes controller creates EndpointSlices
Example EndpointSlice for headless service:

addressType: IPv4
endpoints:
  - addresses: ["10.244.1.12"]  # postgres-0
  - addresses: ["10.244.2.15"]  # postgres-1
CoreDNS reads this object and generates DNS like:

postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.1.12 (pod)
postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.2.15 (pod)
4️⃣ CoreDNS builds per-pod DNS names
Because this is a StatefulSet, Kubernetes assigns predictable pod names:

pod: postgres-0

pod: postgres-1

CoreDNS creates these DNS entries:

postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local  → PodIP
postgres-1.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local  → PodIP
These are created from:

The service name

The service namespace

The pod name

The endpoints mapping

5️⃣ CoreDNS answers DNS queries using in-memory cache
When a client asks:

nslookup postgres-headless
CoreDNS checks:

Is there a service? → Yes (headless)

Does it have EndpointSlice? → Yes

Return A-records of all endpoints

If you ask:

nslookup postgres-0.postgres-headless
It returns exactly one IP — direct pod identity.

🧩 Flow Summary Diagram
[StatefulSet creates pods]
       ↓
[Headless Service created]
       ↓
[Kubernetes creates EndpointSlice with Pod IPs]
       ↓
[CoreDNS (kubernetes plugin) watches API]
       ↓
[Dynamically builds DNS A-records]
       ↓
Client queries DNS → CoreDNS returns Pod IPs
🧠 Why Kubernetes uses EndpointSlice Instead of Endpoints
EndpointSlice scales much better for large clusters.

Endpoints = single object with thousands of IPs (slow)

EndpointSlice = split into chunks of 100 pods per slice (fast)

CoreDNS fully supports both.

🧪 You can inspect EndpointSlices yourself
Check EndpointSlice for your headless service:
kubectl get endpoints postgres-headless
or

kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=postgres-headless -o yaml
This YAML directly translates into DNS A-records.

🟢 Final Summary
✔ CoreDNS doesn’t “create records” manually — it generates DNS dynamically from Kubernetes API objects.
✔ Headless service clusterIP: None causes CoreDNS to create per-pod DNS A-records.
✔ CoreDNS uses EndpointSlices to discover pod IPs.
✔ DNS entries like postgres-0.postgres-headless.svc.cluster.local come from:

the StatefulSet pod name

the headless service name

the EndpointSlice mapping

This is why you get stable, pod-specific DNS names.

